#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Written takeaways for Exodus 1-22."""

TAKEAWAYS = {
    # ---- Chapter 1 ----
    '1:1': "The book of deliverance opens with a family list. The slaves in Egypt are the sons of a man God made promises to.",
    '1:5': "Seventy souls. The nation promised to be numberless as the stars still fits into one sentence.",
    '1:6': "Joseph dies, and the protection dies with him. Favour at court never outlives the man who earned it.",
    '1:7': "The blessing of Genesis keeps working in a foreign country, with nobody supervising it.",
    '1:8': "\"Which knew not Joseph.\" A generation forgets what it owes, and the forgetting is where the oppression begins.",
    '1:9': "Pharaoh names Israel's growth as a threat. From a throne, God's blessing on somebody else looks like danger.",
    '1:10': "\"Let us deal wisely with them.\" The killing of a people is introduced as policy, in the language of prudence.",
    '1:11': "The store cities did get built. Oppression is usually productive, which is most of why it continues.",
    '1:12': "The harder they were pressed, the more they grew. Pharaoh's policy worked against itself from the first day.",
    '1:14': "Morter and brick. The bitterness is spelled out as the ordinary material of an ordinary working day.",
    '1:15': "Shiphrah and Puah are named; the king of Egypt is not. The record keeps the names that mattered.",
    '1:16': "The order is given quietly, to women, to be carried out at the one moment nobody else is watching.",
    '1:17': "\"The midwives feared God.\" One fear displaced another, and that displacement is the whole reason they disobeyed.",
    '1:19': "They answer the king with a story. Scripture reports the deception plainly and never pauses to justify it.",
    '1:20': "God dealt well with the midwives. The kindness lands on the disobedience, not on the compliance.",
    '1:21': "Women who refused to end households were given households of their own.",
    '1:22': "What was whispered to two midwives becomes public decree. The whole nation is conscripted into the killing.",

    # ---- Chapter 2 ----
    '2:1': "No names yet, only the tribe. The deliverer is introduced as anybody's son, from an ordinary marriage.",
    '2:2': "She saw that he was a goodly child and hid him. Faith's first act here is concealment, for as long as it works.",
    '2:3': "The basket is called an ark, the word used of Noah's. Salvation floats again on the water that was meant to kill.",
    '2:4': "The rescue turns on a girl standing far enough off to watch. Nobody counted her as part of the danger.",
    '2:5': "The child is drawn out of the river by the household of the man who ordered him thrown into it.",
    '2:6': "The babe wept, and a state policy collapsed inside one woman's chest.",
    '2:7': "The sister's nerve turns a rescue into a homecoming. She speaks to a princess and gets a hearing.",
    '2:9': "Moses' own mother is paid Egyptian wages to nurse her own son. Providence here has a sense of humour.",
    '2:10': "Drawn out of the water. The name he carries all his life is a summary of what is coming to the nation.",
    '2:11': "\"He looked on their burdens.\" The palace son goes out and decides whose brother he is.",
    '2:12': "He looks for witnesses before he acts. A deliverance that needs an empty horizon is already the wrong kind.",
    '2:13': "The second day the fight is between two Hebrews, and that is the quarrel he cannot settle.",
    '2:14': "\"Who made thee a prince and a judge over us?\" A fair question, and God takes forty years answering it.",
    '2:15': "Egypt's prince ends up sitting by a well in Midian with nothing in his hands at all.",
    '2:17': "The instinct that killed a man in Egypt turns up again as protection of strangers at a trough.",
    '2:19': "\"An Egyptian delivered us.\" The Hebrew rescuer is read off his clothes, by everyone, for years.",
    '2:21': "\"Moses was content to dwell with the man.\" The forty wasted years begin with a man settling down.",
    '2:22': "He names his son after his own homelessness. Moses is still a stranger in the place he has settled.",
    '2:23': "The cry is not addressed to anyone. God hears people who are only groaning, not yet praying.",
    '2:24': "God remembered — not recalled something mislaid, but moved at last on a promise he had never dropped.",
    '2:25': "Four verbs of God and not one act yet. He has looked, and in this book that is where everything turns.",

    # ---- Chapter 3 ----
    '3:1': "He was following sheep, not seeking God. The mountain of God is reached in the course of the day's work.",
    '3:2': "Fire that does not consume. God comes near without destroying the thing he comes near to.",
    '3:4': "God waits for the turning aside before he speaks. The call comes to a man already stopped and looking.",
    '3:5': "The ground was not holy yesterday. Holiness here is God's presence, not a quality the place possessed.",
    '3:6': "God introduces himself by the men he made promises to, and the man he is calling hides his face.",
    '3:7': "Seen, heard, known. God's answer to suffering begins as attention long before it looks like rescue.",
    '3:8': "\"I am come down to deliver them.\" The exodus is God's own descent before it is anybody's errand.",
    '3:10': "God comes down, and then sends a man. In this book the two have never been alternatives.",
    '3:11': "\"Who am I?\" The right question — and God declines to answer it, changing the subject to himself.",
    '3:12': "The answer to \"who am I\" is \"I will be with thee,\" and the confirming sign comes only after the obedience.",
    '3:13': "Moses asks for a name because a people in slavery need to know exactly who is claiming them.",
    '3:14': "I AM THAT I AM. God defines himself by nothing outside himself and will not be pinned down further.",
    '3:15': "The everlasting name is fastened to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. God is known by the history he has kept.",
    '3:16': "The elders are to be told that God has seen what was done to them. None of it went unwitnessed.",
    '3:18': "The request put to Pharaoh is deliberately small. He will refuse even three days, and the refusal will be the point.",
    '3:19': "God tells Moses the mission will fail before it succeeds. The refusal ahead is already inside the plan.",
    '3:20': "Egypt will not be persuaded but struck. The plagues are announced before Pharaoh has said a word.",
    '3:22': "The word is spoil. Israel walks out of Egypt like an army off a battlefield, having fought nothing.",

    # ---- Chapter 4 ----
    '4:1': "The third objection is about other people. Moses has begun defending himself with somebody else's unbelief.",
    '4:2': "\"What is that in thine hand?\" God begins with what the man is already carrying.",
    '4:3': "The thing he had held every day for forty years becomes the thing he runs from.",
    '4:4': "He is told to take it by the tail, the wrong end. Obedience here means handling the danger without precaution.",
    '4:6': "The second sign is worked on his own body. God can make the deliverer unclean, and clean, at a word.",
    '4:8': "God plans for unbelief and supplies a second sign. Patience with slow faith is built into the commission.",
    '4:9': "The third sign turns the Nile to blood — the last argument rehearsed in miniature before the first audience.",
    '4:10': "\"I am slow of speech.\" The objection has moved from the audience to himself, where it always was.",
    '4:11': "God claims the dumb and the deaf and the blind as his own making. A limitation is not an argument against the caller.",
    '4:12': "\"I will be with thy mouth.\" What is promised is not a better speaker but a present God.",
    '4:13': "Out of excuses, Moses drops the arguments and simply asks God to send somebody else.",
    '4:14': "God's anger produces a concession. Aaron is granted, and the concession will cost Israel a golden calf.",
    '4:15': "The chain runs God to Moses to Aaron, and each link says only what was put into it.",
    '4:17': "He goes back to confront an empire holding a shepherd's stick. That is the entire arsenal.",
    '4:18': "Moses asks leave of Jethro without mentioning the bush. The great call is kept to himself.",
    '4:19': "The men who wanted him dead are dead. He has outlasted a whole generation of his enemies by doing nothing.",
    '4:20': "The stick he has carried for forty years is now called the rod of God. Nothing about it changed but whose it is.",
    '4:21': "\"I will harden his heart.\" Scripture makes the claim without softening it, and without excusing Pharaoh either.",
    '4:22': "Israel is God's firstborn before Israel has done anything at all. Sonship here is conferred, not achieved.",
    '4:23': "The last plague is announced in the first message. Pharaoh is told at the outset what refusing will cost him.",
    '4:24': "God moves to kill the man he has just commissioned. The passage is left unexplained, and the text does not flinch.",
    '4:25': "A Midianite wife performs the covenant sign that the deliverer of the covenant people had neglected in his own house.",
    '4:31': "The people believe immediately — the one thing Moses was certain would not happen. It will not survive the next chapter.",
}

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 5 ----
    '5:1': "The demand is not liberty in the abstract but a feast. Israel is to be let go in order to worship.",
    '5:2': "\"Who is the LORD, that I should obey his voice?\" The question is sincere, and the rest of the book answers it.",
    '5:3': "They plead a danger to themselves rather than a right. Slaves ask in the only register they have been left.",
    '5:4': "Pharaoh hears a claim about God and answers it as a labour problem. \"Get you unto your burdens.\"",
    '5:5': "The old arithmetic again: the people are many. Every cruelty in this chapter runs on a census.",
    '5:7': "The answer to a request for three days' rest is more work. That is how such a system defends itself.",
    '5:8': "\"They be idle; therefore they cry.\" Oppression hears a cry for God as laziness and prescribes accordingly.",
    '5:9': "Exhaustion is ordered as a cure for hearing God's word. Overwork is the oldest way of drowning it out.",
    '5:12': "The people are scattered across Egypt hunting stubble, and the quota does not move an inch.",
    '5:14': "The beatings fall on Israel's own officers. Tyranny prefers to be administered by the people it crushes.",
    '5:15': "They take their appeal to the man who signed the order, as though he were not the source of it.",
    '5:16': "\"The fault is in thine own people.\" They still cannot say aloud who is actually doing this to them.",
    '5:17': "He repeats the accusation instead of answering the complaint. Power is under no obligation to make sense.",
    '5:21': "The first result of the deliverance is that the delivered turn on the deliverer and call down judgement on him.",
    '5:22': "Moses takes the complaint straight to God and does not soften it. The prayer is an accusation.",
    '5:23': "\"Neither hast thou delivered thy people at all.\" Obedience made it worse, and Moses says so to God's face.",

    # ---- Chapter 6 ----
    '6:1': "God answers the accusation with a timetable rather than an explanation. \"Now shalt thou see what I will do.\"",
    '6:2': "\"I am the LORD.\" The speech begins and ends on the name, as though the name settled everything in between.",
    '6:3': "The fathers had the promises; this generation is given the name. God discloses himself in instalments.",
    '6:4': "The covenant promised a land to men who never did more than camp in it as strangers.",
    '6:5': "\"I have remembered my covenant.\" The rescue is credited to an old promise, not to the volume of the groaning.",
    '6:6': "Bring out, rid, redeem — every verb in the sentence is God's. Israel's whole part is to be acted upon.",
    '6:7': "Rescue is not the end of it. The goal stated is a people belonging to God and knowing who he is.",
    '6:8': "The land is given on an oath sworn to men long dead. Israel inherits on somebody else's word.",
    '6:9': "They could not hear the promise for the misery. Despair deafens people to the very word that would lift it.",
    '6:12': "Moses reasons from Israel's deafness to Pharaoh's, and lands back on the old excuse about his mouth.",
    '6:20': "The genealogy stops to give Moses parents. The deliverer has a father and mother like anyone else.",
    '6:21': "Korah is entered in the family register without a word of warning. The rebellion is still a generation off.",
    '6:23': "Nadab and Abihu are listed and left. Leviticus will supply the comment this verse withholds.",
    '6:26': "\"These are that Aaron and Moses.\" The list exists to fasten two ordinary men into a real family.",
    '6:30': "The chapter closes where it opened, on Moses objecting. A repeated excuse is its own sort of unbelief.",

    # ---- Chapter 7 ----
    '7:1': "\"I have made thee a god to Pharaoh.\" The man with the slow tongue is given a standing he never asked for.",
    '7:3': "The hardening and the multiplied signs are named in one breath. The delay is what makes room for the wonders.",
    '7:4': "Pharaoh's refusal is what opens Egypt to judgement. God is not obstructed by it; he is working through it.",
    '7:5': "Egypt too is meant to come out of this knowing the LORD. The judgement has a teaching purpose beyond Israel.",
    '7:7': "Eighty and eighty-three. Two full lifetimes were spent before the first word was spoken to Pharaoh.",
    '7:9': "Pharaoh's demand for a miracle is anticipated and answered in advance. Nothing here catches God unprepared.",
    '7:10': "Aaron casts the rod, not Moses. The brother given as a concession is doing the visible work.",
    '7:11': "Egypt can match the first sign. A wonder on its own proves less than we would like it to prove.",
    '7:12': "The rods are equal until one swallows the others. The difference was never in the trick.",
    '7:13': "\"As the LORD had said.\" The refrain records confirmation, not surprise; even the refusal was foretold.",
    '7:15': "Moses meets him at the river at dawn — at the water Egypt trusted, before the day's business begins.",
    '7:16': "\"Hitherto thou wouldest not hear.\" Every plague is preceded by one more chance to obey.",
    '7:18': "The Nile that fed Egypt and swallowed Hebrew sons is the first thing struck.",
    '7:20': "It is done in the sight of Pharaoh and his servants. Nobody afterwards will be able to say it did not happen.",
    '7:22': "The magicians answer the plague by producing more of it. Imitation can copy a judgement without undoing it.",
    '7:23': "He turns and goes into his house. The most dangerous response to God in this book is not rage but indifference.",
    '7:24': "Ordinary Egyptians dig for water while the king ignores the whole thing. Rulers seldom drink their own policy.",
    '7:25': "Seven days of blood, and then a pause. God's judgements come with intervals built into them.",

    # ---- Chapter 8 ----
    '8:1': "\"That they may serve me.\" Israel is not being freed from service but transferred to another master.",
    '8:3': "The plague is described by where it will be — the bed, the oven, the kneadingtrough. Judgement reaches the kitchen.",
    '8:6': "The river that was blood a week ago now breeds the next plague. Egypt's water keeps turning against Egypt.",
    '8:7': "The magicians can add frogs and cannot remove one. Counterfeit power only ever enlarges the problem.",
    '8:8': "Pharaoh asks for prayer from men he despises. Pressure manufactures a piety that lasts as long as the pressure.",
    '8:9': "Moses lets Pharaoh name the hour, so that no one afterwards can call the relief a coincidence.",
    '8:10': "\"To morrow.\" Given the choice of any moment, he takes one more night with the frogs rather than seem desperate.",
    '8:12': "Moses prays for the man destroying his people, because he said he would. The promise binds him.",
    '8:13': "The LORD did according to the word of Moses. A man's prayer is taken that seriously in this book.",
    '8:14': "Heaps of dead frogs, and the land stank. Being delivered from a plague leaves a mess of its own.",
    '8:15': "\"When Pharaoh saw that there was respite, he hardened his heart.\" It is the relief that hardens him, not the plague.",
    '8:17': "Egypt's own dust becomes the plague. There is no neutral ground left in the country to stand on.",
    '8:18': "The imitators reach their limit at lice. Whatever they had been doing, it was never the same thing.",
    '8:19': "\"This is the finger of God.\" Pharaoh's own technicians say it plainly, and he still will not hear them.",
    '8:22': "God draws a line through Egypt at Goshen. From here the difference between the two peoples is visible weather.",
    '8:25': "The first compromise offered: worship, but stay in the country. Pharaoh will concede anything but departure.",
    '8:26': "Moses refuses on the ground that real worship offends the neighbours. It cannot be done discreetly in Egypt.",
    '8:27': "\"As he shall command us.\" The terms of worship are no more Israel's to set than Pharaoh's.",
    '8:28': "\"Only ye shall not go very far away.\" The second compromise keeps the distance short enough to reverse.",
    '8:29': "Moses names the deceit before it occurs. He has stopped expecting this king to keep his word.",
    '8:31': "There remained not one fly. The relief is complete, and given to a man who will break faith by evening.",
    '8:32': "\"At this time also.\" The relapse has become a pattern, and the narrator records it without comment.",

    # ---- Chapter 9 ----
    '9:3': "The blow falls on Egypt's livestock. The judgements move inward by stages, from the water to the living.",
    '9:4': "The line is drawn again, now through the herds. Israel's property is covered by the same distinction.",
    '9:5': "A set time is announced beforehand. The plague is dated so that nobody can afterwards call it weather.",
    '9:7': "He sends, verifies that Israel lost nothing, and hardens anyway. Evidence was never what he lacked.",
    '9:8': "Ashes from the furnace where slaves baked bricks are flung into the air. The oppression becomes the instrument.",
    '9:9': "The ash becomes dust and the dust becomes boils on every body in Egypt. The whole land turns septic.",
    '9:11': "The magicians cannot even stand there. The opposition is not refuted so much as taken off the field.",
    '9:12': "Here the text first says the LORD hardened him. Pharaoh's own hardening came first and is not cancelled by it.",
    '9:14': "\"Upon thine heart.\" The plagues are aimed at the one place in Egypt that has not yet given way.",
    '9:15': "God states plainly that he could have ended this at the start. The restraint is deliberate, not weakness.",
    '9:16': "\"For this cause have I raised thee up.\" Paul will quote it; here it means a tyrant is made to serve God's fame.",
    '9:17': "The charge is pride — exalting himself against a people who own nothing and have no army.",
    '9:19': "In the middle of a judgement God gives instructions for escaping it. Even the warning is mercy.",
    '9:20': "Some of Pharaoh's own servants feared the word and moved. The dividing line runs through Egypt as well.",
    '9:21': "The man who regarded not the word left his servants in the field. Ignoring God costs other people their lives.",
    '9:23': "Thunder, hail, and fire running along the ground. The sky itself is described as being at war with Egypt.",
    '9:24': "Nothing in Egypt's memory compares. The oldest nation on earth is outdone in a single afternoon.",
    '9:27': "\"I have sinned this time: the LORD is righteous.\" A confession can be word-perfect and still only weather-deep.",
    '9:28': "\"For it is enough.\" He wants the thunder stopped; he has asked for nothing else to be changed.",
    '9:29': "Stopping the storm has the same purpose as sending it — that he may know the earth is the LORD's.",
    '9:30': "Moses answers the confession by saying he does not believe it, and then goes out and prays for the man.",
    '9:31': "The narrator pauses to say which crops were in the ear. The ruin is dated by the farming calendar, exactly.",
    '9:32': "The wheat survives, which leaves something for the locusts. These judgements are measured, not spent.",
    '9:34': "He sins more as soon as the rain stops. Mercy given to an unchanged man does not soften him; it emboldens him.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 10 ----
    '10:1': "Now the servants' hearts are hardened along with the king's. A nation follows its ruler into his blindness.",
    '10:2': "The plagues are given so that grandchildren will have something to be told. The rescue is made into a story on purpose.",
    '10:3': "\"How long wilt thou refuse to humble thyself before me?\" The whole contest is named as a matter of pride.",
    '10:5': "The locusts are sent for whatever the hail left. These judgements are arranged to finish each other's work.",
    '10:6': "He delivers the sentence and walks out without waiting for a reply. The negotiating has ended.",
    '10:7': "His own courtiers ask whether he has noticed that Egypt is destroyed. Obstinacy is the last thing to feel a loss.",
    '10:8': "\"Who are they that shall go?\" The bargaining is now about who counts, and Pharaoh wants hostages left behind.",
    '10:9': "\"We will go with our young and with our old.\" Nobody is surplus to the feast; a whole household worships or none does.",
    '10:10': "He warns them that their own plan will ruin them. A threat dressed up as concern is still a threat.",
    '10:11': "\"Go now ye that are men.\" Worship without the children is worship with no next generation in it.",
    '10:13': "The locusts arrive on an east wind blowing a day and a night. God's extraordinary work usually comes by ordinary means.",
    '10:15': "Not any green thing left in all the land. The richest country in the world is stripped down to bare ground.",
    '10:16': "\"The LORD your God.\" Even while confessing, he keeps the LORD at arm's length as somebody else's God.",
    '10:17': "He asks pardon for a sin he intends to repeat, and what he actually wants lifted is the death, not the guilt.",
    '10:19': "The sea that will drown his army first swallows his locusts. The ending has been rehearsed in front of him.",
    '10:21': "Darkness that could be felt. The plague before the last one takes Egypt back behind the first day of creation.",
    '10:23': "Nobody rose from his place for three days. Egypt sits paralysed in the dark while Israel has light indoors.",
    '10:24': "The last offer: take the children, leave the cattle. He is still trying to keep a rope on them.",
    '10:25': "Moses asks the oppressor to supply the sacrifices. He will not leave Egypt owed anything or owing anything.",
    '10:26': "\"We know not with what we must serve the LORD, until we come thither.\" Obedience sets out before the instructions arrive.",
    '10:28': "The king who would not let them go now orders them out of the room. He ends an interview he could not win.",
    '10:29': "\"Thou hast spoken well.\" Moses agrees with the threat; when they next speak, Pharaoh will have sent for him.",

    # ---- Chapter 11 ----
    '11:1': "One plague more, and then he will not merely release them — he will thrust them out.",
    '11:2': "The slaves are told to ask their neighbours for silver and gold. Back wages, collected the night before leaving.",
    '11:3': "Moses is now great in Egyptian eyes. The man who fled as a murderer is respected in Pharaoh's own household.",
    '11:4': "\"About midnight will I go out into the midst of Egypt.\" No messenger is named; God says he will come himself.",
    '11:5': "From the throne to the maidservant at the mill. Death that night will not observe the social order.",
    '11:7': "Not a dog will move his tongue against Israel. The difference God makes is drawn down to that detail.",
    '11:8': "He goes out from Pharaoh in a great anger. The text records the prophet's fury without apologising for it.",

    # ---- Chapter 12 ----
    '12:2': "The calendar is restarted at the redemption. A people's year now begins where God rescued them.",
    '12:3': "A lamb for a house. The unit of this rescue is the household, not the individual.",
    '12:4': "Neighbours share a lamb so that none is wasted and no one is left out. The provision is sized to the eaters.",
    '12:5': "Without blemish. The substitute must have nothing wrong with it, which is the entire point of a substitute.",
    '12:6': "The whole congregation kills it at one hour. Every household in Israel has blood on its hands that evening.",
    '12:7': "The blood goes on the outside of the door, where the family inside cannot see it. It was never for their reassurance.",
    '12:8': "Bitter herbs are written into the meal. The remembering is not permitted to turn sentimental.",
    '12:10': "Nothing is kept back for the morning. This meal belongs to one night and cannot be stored.",
    '12:11': "They eat with shoes on and staff in hand. The supper is taken by people who expect to be gone by daylight.",
    '12:12': "\"Against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgment.\" The plagues are finally named for what they were.",
    '12:13': "\"When I see the blood, I will pass over you.\" Safety rests on what God sees, not on anything felt inside the house.",
    '12:14': "The night is made a feast before it has happened. The rescue is commanded to be remembered in advance.",
    '12:15': "Leaven is put out for seven days. One night's haste becomes a week of deliberate practice, every year.",
    '12:22': "None of them may step outside until morning. That night safety is a matter of staying under the blood, not of courage.",
    '12:23': "God himself keeps the destroyer out. The blood does not repel anything; it marks a house God undertakes to defend.",
    '12:26': "The rite is built to provoke a child's question. Worship nobody asks about has quietly stopped teaching.",
    '12:27': "The people bowed and worshipped before anything had happened. That evening's obedience is entirely on trust.",
    '12:29': "The prisoner's firstborn dies with the prince's. Egypt's poorest are not spared for having been poor.",
    '12:30': "\"There was not a house where there was not one dead.\" The night Israel was spared was not a cheap night.",
    '12:31': "He concedes every point at once, in the dark, in his own grief. The delay bought him absolutely nothing.",
    '12:32': "\"And bless me also.\" The man who asked who the LORD was ends by begging a blessing from him.",
    '12:33': "The Egyptians push them out in a panic. In the end the exodus happens at Egypt's own urging.",
    '12:34': "They leave with unrisen dough on their shoulders. The feast will commemorate a departure too quick to bake.",
    '12:36': "Slaves walk out paid. Four centuries of unwaged labour are settled in a single night of asking.",
    '12:37': "Six hundred thousand men on foot. The seventy souls of the first chapter have become a nation.",
    '12:38': "A mixed multitude goes up with them. The redeemed community is not ethnically tidy from its first day.",
    '12:39': "They had prepared no provisions. Everything from here forward depends on God feeding them daily.",
    '12:40': "Four hundred and thirty years. God's promise was keeping a schedule that nobody alive could see.",
    '12:41': "\"Even the selfsame day.\" After four centuries of apparent silence, the deliverance is punctual to the day.",
    '12:43': "The meal has a boundary drawn round it. Not everyone present that night belonged to the covenant.",
    '12:44': "A slave bought with money may eat it. Rank in the household decides nothing here; the covenant sign decides.",
    '12:46': "Not a bone of it broken. John will stand at the cross and remember this line.",
    '12:48': "A stranger may come in on the same terms as a native. The door is narrow, and it is genuinely open.",

    # ---- Chapter 13 ----
    '13:2': "Every firstborn belongs to God because every firstborn was spared. The claim rests on the rescue.",
    '13:3': "\"Remember this day.\" The first command given to a free people concerns memory.",
    '13:5': "The feast is commanded for a land they have not reached. Obedience is scheduled into a future God guarantees.",
    '13:8': "The father tells it as what God did for him, not for his ancestors. The rescue is kept first-hand.",
    '13:9': "Hand, eyes, mouth. The law is to be carried where the working and the looking and the talking happen.",
    '13:12': "\"The males shall be the LORD's.\" What God saved from death he keeps a standing claim on.",
    '13:13': "The unclean animal is bought back with a lamb or its neck is broken. Either way something dies; redemption is not optional.",
    '13:14': "The catechism runs both ways: the child asks, and the father is expected to have the answer ready.",
    '13:15': "Israel's sons are redeemed instead of offered. Behind every Hebrew boy who lived stands a firstborn who died.",
    '13:17': "God avoids the short road because they could not yet face a war. He leads according to what they can bear.",
    '13:18': "They go up harnessed, in the order of an army — an army that is not going to have to fight.",
    '13:19': "Joseph's bones travel with them. A man dead four hundred years had believed this morning would come.",
    '13:21': "The guidance is visible, constant, and not a map. They are led one day and one night at a time.",
    '13:22': "The pillar was never taken away. Whatever else failed in the wilderness, the presence did not.",

    # ---- Chapter 14 ----
    '14:2': "God tells them to camp with their backs to the water. The trap they are in is his own arrangement.",
    '14:3': "God predicts what Pharaoh will conclude. The bait is Israel looking thoroughly lost.",
    '14:4': "\"I will be honoured upon Pharaoh.\" The sea is not only Israel's rescue; God intends to be known by it.",
    '14:5': "Once the grief cools the economics return. He misses the labour more than he fears the God who took it.",
    '14:7': "Six hundred chariots against people on foot with their children. The odds are stated so the outcome cannot be mistaken.",
    '14:8': "They went out with an high hand, and were hunted anyway. Confidence is not the same thing as safety.",
    '14:10': "They cry to the LORD and turn on Moses in the same breath. Fear can pray and rage at once.",
    '14:11': "\"Because there were no graves in Egypt.\" Panic makes them sarcastic about the God who delivered them last week.",
    '14:12': "Days into freedom, slavery already looks like the safer arrangement. It will not be the last time.",
    '14:13': "\"Stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD.\" The only order given is to stop doing anything.",
    '14:14': "The LORD fights and they hold their peace. Their entire contribution to this deliverance is silence.",
    '14:15': "Having said stand still, God now says go forward. Waiting on God was never meant to be a permanent posture.",
    '14:16': "The sea is divided by a man holding out a stick. God insists on using the instrument he gave.",
    '14:19': "The pillar that led them moves round behind them. Guidance turns into a rearguard when that is what is needed.",
    '14:20': "The same cloud is darkness on one side and light on the other. One presence, two entirely different nights.",
    '14:21': "The sea is opened by a wind blowing all night long. The miracle takes hours and works through the weather.",
    '14:22': "They walk between walls of water. Every step is taken inside the thing that could drown them.",
    '14:23': "Egypt drives its chariots onto a dry seabed. Hard men will follow God's people into places only faith survives.",
    '14:24': "The LORD looked, and an army fell into confusion. No weapon is mentioned anywhere in the verse.",
    '14:25': "\"The LORD fighteth for them.\" The confession Pharaoh refused to make is made by his drowning soldiers.",
    '14:27': "The water simply goes back to being water. The judgement is creation resuming its ordinary course.",
    '14:28': "There remained not so much as one. The power that held Israel four hundred years is gone before breakfast.",
    '14:30': "They see the bodies on the shore. This salvation is something Israel stood and watched happen to somebody else.",
    '14:31': "Fear and faith arrive together, and the faith is put in the LORD and in his servant Moses.",
})

TAKEAWAYS.update({
    # ---- Chapter 15 ----
    '15:1': "The first song in Scripture comes after a rescue. Israel learns to sing standing on the far bank.",
    '15:2': "\"He is become my salvation.\" Not gave it — became it. The rescue and the rescuer are the same thing.",
    '15:3': "\"The LORD is a man of war: the LORD is his name.\" Israel's worship is not embarrassed that God fought.",
    '15:4': "The chosen captains are named in the boast. The song is precise about exactly what God overthrew.",
    '15:5': "They sank as a stone. The fastest machines in the world went straight to the bottom.",
    '15:6': "Twice the right hand of the LORD. The song credits one hand for the whole day, and it is not Moses'.",
    '15:7': "Wrath is pictured as fire in dry stubble — quick, total, and costing the one who sends it nothing.",
    '15:8': "Last night's east wind is sung as the blast of God's nostrils. Faith reads the weather back to its source.",
    '15:9': "Six boasts in one verse, every one beginning with I. The enemy is quoted at length so he condemns himself.",
    '15:10': "One breath of God answers six boasts, and they sink as lead. The contest was never close.",
    '15:11': "\"Who is like unto thee, glorious in holiness, fearful in praises?\" The song's centre is God, not the victory.",
    '15:13': "Mercy led them out, strength will guide them in. Two verses past the sea the song is already looking forward.",
    '15:14': "The news runs ahead of them. Before Israel reaches Canaan the rumour of this God has arrived there.",
    '15:16': "\"Which thou hast purchased.\" Israel is sung about as bought property, and in this book that is good news.",
    '15:17': "The destination named is a sanctuary, not a farm. They were brought out in order to be brought in.",
    '15:18': "The song ends on a reign rather than a victory. The drowning of an army was only an instance of it.",
    '15:20': "Miriam is called a prophetess and leads the women out with timbrels. The sister who watched the basket is still here.",
    '15:22': "Three days after the sea, no water. Seventy-two hours separate the song from the next crisis.",
    '15:23': "They reach water at last and cannot drink it. The disappointment is sharper for the arrival.",
    '15:24': "The murmuring starts here, and it does not stop for forty years.",
    '15:25': "God's answer is a tree thrown into the water, and the whole episode is then called a test of them.",
    '15:26': "\"I am the LORD that healeth thee\" — said beside bitter water he has this moment made sweet.",
    '15:27': "Twelve wells and seventy palms, one for each tribe and elder. The abundance comes straight after the bitterness.",

    # ---- Chapter 16 ----
    '16:2': "The whole congregation murmurs. It is no longer a faction; complaint has become the community's own voice.",
    '16:3': "Memory rewrites Egypt as a place of full bread. Slavery looks comfortable to people who are hungry now.",
    '16:4': "The bread falls daily on purpose. The test is whether they will consent to live at the rate God supplies.",
    '16:5': "The sabbath is built into the food supply before it is written on any tablet of stone.",
    '16:7': "\"What are we?\" Moses refuses to be the target of the complaint, and refuses to be its answer.",
    '16:8': "\"Your murmurings are not against us, but against the LORD.\" Complaint about the arrangements is about the one who arranged them.",
    '16:10': "The answer to the murmuring is the glory of the LORD appearing in the cloud. God comes toward the grumbling.",
    '16:12': "Flesh at evening, bread at morning — and the stated purpose is that they should know him, not merely be fed.",
    '16:13': "The quails came at evening and the dew at morning. The promise was kept the same day it was spoken.",
    '16:14': "The bread of heaven looks like frost on the ground. Provision generally arrives looking like nothing much.",
    '16:15': "Manna means \"what is it?\" They ate it forty years and never stopped calling it a question.",
    '16:18': "He that gathered much had nothing over. Paul will quote this line to the Corinthians about money.",
    '16:20': "Hoarded manna bred worms and stank. The bread keeps only on the terms it was given.",
    '16:21': "It melted when the sun grew hot. Daily bread comes with a window, and the window is early.",
    '16:23': "Rest is announced, and the baking for it is done the day before. Sabbath takes planning.",
    '16:24': "The manna that bred worms on Tuesday keeps clean overnight on Friday. The rule was never about the food.",
    '16:27': "Some went out on the seventh day and found nothing. Unbelief will work through a rest rather than trust the arithmetic.",
    '16:28': "\"How long refuse ye to keep my commandments?\" God puts to Israel the question he had put to Pharaoh.",
    '16:30': "\"So the people rested.\" Three words for a habit that has taken a whole chapter to teach.",
    '16:31': "The wilderness bread tasted like wafers made with honey. The monotony they complained of was never bland.",
    '16:32': "A jar is kept so that settled children can see what their parents lived on out here.",
    '16:35': "Forty years of the same miracle. Most of God's provision stops looking miraculous by the second week.",

    # ---- Chapter 17 ----
    '17:1': "They camped where the LORD told them and found no water. Obedient travel is no guarantee of supply.",
    '17:2': "Moses hears the complaint as a test of God rather than of himself. He is learning whose quarrel this is.",
    '17:3': "The charge has hardened: he brought them out to kill them. Thirst makes a rescuer look like a murderer.",
    '17:4': "The deliverer is nearly stoned by the delivered. Leading these people has become physically dangerous.",
    '17:5': "The rod that turned the Nile to blood is called for again. The instrument of judgement is about to bring water.",
    '17:6': "God stands on the rock that is struck. The blow falls where he is, and the people drink.",
    '17:7': "\"Is the LORD among us, or not?\" They asked it standing under the pillar of cloud.",
    '17:8': "Amalek attacks as soon as the water is given. Relief from one trouble is not the end of trouble.",
    '17:11': "The fighting in the valley rises and falls with a pair of hands on a hill above it.",
    '17:12': "The great man's arms are held up by two other men. Even this intercession had to be propped from outside.",
    '17:14': "The first command in Scripture to write something in a book concerns a people God means to blot out.",
    '17:15': "He names the altar after the banner rather than after the win. The credit is built into the name.",
    '17:16': "The war is declared perpetual. Scripture does not expect every enmity to be resolved inside history.",

    # ---- Chapter 18 ----
    '18:1': "A Midianite priest hears what God has done and comes. The report of the exodus is already travelling.",
    '18:2': "Zipporah had been sent home. The text mentions it in passing and never explains the strain in the marriage.",
    '18:4': "Eliezer's name records a rescue from Pharaoh's sword. Moses named his children after his own history.",
    '18:7': "Moses bows to his father in law. The man who faced down Pharaoh still defers inside a family tent.",
    '18:8': "He tells the travail by the way as well as the deliverance. Both belong in an honest account.",
    '18:11': "\"Now I know that the LORD is greater than all gods.\" A pagan priest's confession is recorded without embarrassment.",
    '18:12': "Israel's elders eat bread with a Midianite at his sacrifice. The table is already wider than the tribe.",
    '18:13': "One man judging a whole nation from morning to evening. The scene is described plainly before it is criticised.",
    '18:14': "The question comes from an outsider who is not impressed by how hard Moses is working.",
    '18:16': "He is teaching the statutes as well as settling the cases. That is the part of the work worth protecting.",
    '18:17': "\"The thing that thou doest is not good.\" Sincere overwork is corrected here as a fault, not admired.",
    '18:18': "\"Thou wilt surely wear away.\" The warning is for the people waiting as much as for the man exhausting himself.",
    '18:19': "What cannot be handed on is named first: standing before God for them. Everything else can be shared out.",
    '18:20': "Teaching comes before judging. Fewer disputes arrive when the people already know the law.",
    '18:21': "The qualifications are character, not expertise — and a judge who loves money is the one thing excluded outright.",
    '18:22': "They shall bear the burden with him. This is sharing the load, not shedding it.",
    '18:23': "The test of the reform is whether the people go home in peace, not whether Moses feels less tired.",
    '18:24': "The man who speaks with God face to face takes practical advice from his wife's father, and acts on it.",
    '18:27': "Jethro goes back to his own land. He is not absorbed into Israel, and nothing between them is lessened by it.",
})
